    "created_at": "2017-11-03T04:35:28Z",
    "updated_at": "2017-11-03T04:35:42Z",
    "body": "@Jedipedia , \r\n> It will only work if JavaScript is enabled, otherwise you just see a blank page.\r\n\r\nWhen you open SVG on legacy browsers it would not be rendered, but will in new browsers. Does it prevent for browsers to render it without embedding into HTML? This is a proposal for a standard, which eventually be picked up by browsers. Same way as SVG.\r\n\r\n> It will not work with web crawlers\r\n\r\nNot all content meant for crawlers. Especially JS. robot.txt is designed to cover this case. \r\nAlso there is a \"accept\" http header which server could honor if the content meant for crawler, rendering content on server side.\r\n\r\n> there is no need to execute JavaScript during page load...\r\n\r\nWhy you decide? Others have different opinion and here is highlighted that de-facto JS is a primary content on many sites. Ignoring this fact would not make a standard stronger.\r\n",
